.TH OTPME.CONF 5 "2026-11-09" "OTPme 0.3.0a288" "OTPme Manual"
.SH NAME
otpme.conf \- OTPme configuration file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I /etc/otpme/otpme.conf
.br
.I ~/.otpme/otpme.conf
.SH DESCRIPTION
.na
.nh
.B otpme.conf
sets how OTPme runs on this system: the index and cache, the daemons, logging, backups and more.
The settings of the realm, its sites and objects are not here; they are config parameters of the objects, see
.BR otpme (7).
.PP
Each line is \fINAME\fR=\fIvalue\fR, e.g.
.RS
.PP
.B LOGLEVEL=\(dqINFO\(dq
.RE
.PP
The value can be in double or single quotes, or without them.
\fBTrue\fR and \fBFalse\fR are yes and no, a list is comma separated.
A line that starts with \fB#\fR is a comment, and so is the rest of a line after a value and a \fB#\fR.
An empty value is the same as no line: the default stays.
A line OTPme cannot read stops it with a message that names the line.
A parameter it does not know gives a message and is ignored.
.PP
A command reads the file each time it runs.
The daemons read it when they start, and again on \fBotpme\-controld reload\fR.
.PP
A user can set some parameters for themselves, in \fI~/.otpme/otpme.conf\fR; that one wins for them.
Those parameters say so below.
.PP
\fI/etc/otpme/otpme.conf.dist\fR has all parameters with a comment on each, see \fBFILES\fR.
.hy
.ad
.SH GENERAL
.na
.nh
.TP
.B BIN_DIR
The directory of the OTPme commands (default: \fI/usr/local/bin\fR).
\fBotpme\-agent\fR and \fBotpme\-controld\fR are started from there, and scripts find the commands there: it comes first in their \fBPATH\fR and is in \fBOTPME_BIN_DIR\fR.
.TP
.B TIMEZONE
The time zone of the certificates a CA issues (default: \fBEurope/Berlin\fR).
.TP
.B LANGUAGE
The language of the output (default: \fBenv\fR).
\fBenv\fR takes it from \fBLC_ALL\fR, \fBLC_MESSAGES\fR or \fBLANG\fR, the first one set; without any it is \fBen\fR.
There are \fBen\fR, \fBde\fR, \fBes\fR, \fBfr\fR and \fBit\fR; any other gives a message and \fBen\fR.
What the servers answer is in the language of the user, if one was set with \fBotpme\-user language\fR.
A user can set it in \fI~/.otpme/otpme.conf\fR as well.
.TP
.B LOG_LANGUAGE
The language of the log messages (default: \fBen\fR).
.TP
.B USER\fR, \fBGROUP
The system user and group that own the files of OTPme and that the daemons run as (default: \fBotpme\fR and \fBotpme\fR).
.TP
.B HANDLE_FILES_DIRS
Whether OTPme makes the directories and files it needs and sets their owner and mode (default: \fBTrue\fR).
With \fBFalse\fR the package or the administrator has to see to that; a missing one is then an error.
.TP
.B PINENTRY
The pinentry program (default: \fI/usr/bin/pinentry\fR).
\fBotpme\-agent\fR asks for the PIN and for a confirmation with it, and \fBotpme\-pinentry\fR wraps it.
A user can set it in \fI~/.otpme/otpme.conf\fR as well.
.TP
.B USE_MGMTD_SOCKET
Whether a command goes to the local mgmtd through its socket when nobody is logged in, as the global option \fB\-\-socket\fR does (default: \fBFalse\fR).
Without it, such a command fails with "Not logged in".
Only on a node, where mgmtd runs.
A user can set it in \fI~/.otpme/otpme.conf\fR as well.
.TP
.B USE_PAGER
Whether long output of a command is shown through a pager, only ever on a terminal (default: \fBauto\fR).
\fBauto\fR pages when the output does not fit the terminal, \fBTrue\fR always, \fBFalse\fR never.
The pager is the one \fBPAGER\fR names, else \fBpager\fR or \fBless\fR.
A user can set it in \fI~/.otpme/otpme.conf\fR as well.
.hy
.ad
.SH INDEX DATABASE
.na
.nh
The index is the database next to the object files that OTPme searches.
It can always be built again from the files, see \fBindex rebuild\fR in
.BR otpme\-tool (1).
PostgreSQL and MySQL run as a server of their own, with their files in \fI/var/lib/otpme/index/\fR and a socket in \fI/var/run/otpme/sockets/\fR; OTPme starts it and writes its configuration.
.TP
.B INDEX
The type: \fBsqlite3\fR, \fBpostgres\fR or \fBmysql\fR.
It has to be set; without it no command runs.
SQLite is meant for hosts with few objects; do not use it on nodes.
Run \fBotpme\-tool index stop\fR before changing it.
The index of the old type is not carried over.
.TP
.B AUTOSTART_INDEX
Whether \fBotpme\-controld\fR starts the PostgreSQL or MySQL server when it starts, and stops it when it stops (default: \fBTrue\fR).
With \fBFalse\fR the server has to run already, else the daemons do not start: "Index not started".
The global option \fB\-\-no\-index\-start\fR does the same for one start.
SQLite needs no server.
.SS SQLite3
The index is the file \fI/var/lib/otpme/index/objects.sqlite\fR.
It is meant for hosts with few objects, not for nodes: a node holds all objects of the site, and its daemons write to the index at the same time.
.TP
.B SQLITE3_BIN
The \fBsqlite3\fR program that \fBotpme\-tool index cli\fR opens (default: \fBsqlite3\fR).
.TP
.B SQLITE3_PRAGMA_SYNCHRONOUS
How often SQLite waits for the disk: \fBOFF\fR, \fBNORMAL\fR, \fBFULL\fR or \fBEXTRA\fR (default: \fBOFF\fR).
With \fBOFF\fR a crash of the machine can damage the index; \fBotpme\-tool index rebuild\fR builds it again.
.SS PostgreSQL
.TP
.B POSTGRES_PG_CTL_BIN\fR, \fBPOSTGRES_PSQL_BIN\fR, \fBPOSTGRES_CREATEDB_BIN\fR, \fBPOSTGRES_CREATEUSER_BIN\fR, \fBPOSTGRES_PG_ISREADY_BIN
The PostgreSQL programs (default: \fBpg_ctl\fR, \fBpsql\fR, \fBcreatedb\fR, \fBcreateuser\fR and \fBpg_isready\fR, found in \fBPATH\fR).
On Debian \fBpg_ctl\fR is not in \fBPATH\fR: give the full path, e.g. \fI/usr/lib/postgresql/15/bin/pg_ctl\fR.
.TP
.B POSTGRES_DBAPI
The Python driver: \fBpsycopg2\fR (default), \fBpg8000\fR, \fBpygresql\fR or \fBpsycopg2cffi\fR.
.TP
.B POSTGRES_SOCKET_DIR
The directory of the socket (default: \fI/var/run/otpme/sockets/postgres\fR).
.PP
These go into the \fIpostgresql.conf\fR that OTPme writes, as the PostgreSQL setting of the same name in lower case: \fBPOSTGRES_WORK_MEM\fR is \fBwork_mem\fR.
What they do is in the PostgreSQL documentation.
The defaults:
\fBPOSTGRES_MAX_CONNECTIONS\fR 500,
\fBPOSTGRES_SHARED_BUFFERS\fR 512MB,
\fBPOSTGRES_WORK_MEM\fR 128MB,
\fBPOSTGRES_MAINTENANCE_WORK_MEM\fR 256MB,
\fBPOSTGRES_EFFECTIVE_CACHE_SIZE\fR 512MB,
\fBPOSTGRES_DYNAMIC_SHARED_MEMORY_TYPE\fR posix,
\fBPOSTGRES_FSYNC\fR on,
\fBPOSTGRES_SYNCHRONOUS_COMMIT\fR off,
\fBPOSTGRES_RANDOM_PAGE_COST\fR 1.0,
\fBPOSTGRES_ENABLE_HASHJOIN\fR off,
\fBPOSTGRES_AUTOVACUUM\fR on,
\fBPOSTGRES_AUTOVACUUM_WORK_MEM\fR \-1,
\fBPOSTGRES_AUTOVACUUM_NAPTIME\fR 1min,
\fBPOSTGRES_AUTOVACUUM_MAX_WORKERS\fR 5,
\fBPOSTGRES_AUTOVACUUM_FREEZE_MAX_AGE\fR 100000,
\fBPOSTGRES_AUTOVACUUM_VACUUM_THRESHOLD\fR 50,
\fBPOSTGRES_AUTOVACUUM_ANALYZE_THRESHOLD\fR 50,
\fBPOSTGRES_DATESTYLE\fR "iso, mdy",
\fBPOSTGRES_TIMEZONE\fR and \fBPOSTGRES_LOG_TIMEZONE\fR Europe/Berlin,
\fBPOSTGRES_DEFAULT_TEXT_SEARCH_CONFIG\fR pg_catalog.english.
.PP
A file \fI/etc/otpme/postgresql.conf\fR is used instead of the one OTPme writes.
Then none of the settings above apply.
.SS MySQL
.TP
.B MYSQLD_BIN\fR, \fBMYSQL_BIN\fR, \fBMYSQL_ADMIN_BIN\fR, \fBMYSQL_INSTALL_DB
The MySQL programs (default: \fI/usr/sbin/mysqld\fR, then \fBmysql\fR, \fBmysqladmin\fR and \fBmysql_install_db\fR, found in \fBPATH\fR).
.TP
.B MYSQL_DBAPI
The Python driver: \fBpymysql\fR (default), \fBmysqldb\fR or \fBcymysql\fR.
.TP
.B MYSQL_SOCKET_DIR
The directory of the socket \fImysqld.sock\fR (default: \fI/var/run/otpme/sockets/mysql\fR).
.PP
These go into the \fImysql.cnf\fR that OTPme writes, as the MySQL setting of that name: \fBMYSQL_KEY_BUFFER_SIZE\fR is \fBkey_buffer_size\fR, \fBMYSQL_CHARACTER_SET_SERVER\fR is \fBcharacter\-set\-server\fR.
The defaults:
\fBMYSQL_MAX_CONNECTIONS\fR 512,
\fBMYSQL_KEY_BUFFER_SIZE\fR 160M,
\fBMYSQL_THREAD_STACK\fR 192K,
\fBMYSQL_MAX_ALLOWED_PACKET\fR 512M,
\fBMYSQL_THREAD_CACHE_SIZE\fR 8,
\fBMYSQL_QUERY_CACHE_LIMIT\fR 10M,
\fBMYSQL_QUERY_CACHE_SIZE\fR 160M,
\fBMYSQL_EXPIRE_LOGS_DAYS\fR 10,
\fBMYSQL_MAX_BINLOG_SIZE\fR 100M,
\fBMYSQL_BINLOG_FORMAT\fR ROW,
\fBMYSQL_CHARACTER_SET_SERVER\fR utf8mb4,
\fBMYSQL_COLLATION_SERVER\fR utf8mb4_general_ci,
\fBMYSQL_MYISAM_RECOVER_OPTIONS\fR BACKUP.
.PP
\fBMYSQL_BINLOG_FORMAT\fR must be \fBROW\fR or \fBMIXED\fR.
The index reads with READ COMMITTED, and MySQL refuses every write with \fBSTATEMENT\fR.
.PP
A file \fI/etc/otpme/mysql.cnf\fR is used instead of the one OTPme writes.
Then none of the settings above apply.
.hy
.ad
.SH CACHE
.na
.nh
The cache holds what the processes of a host or node share, in Redis or memcached: the state of the cluster and of running jobs, and the results of some lookups, such as the roles and tokens of a user and password hashes.
It runs as a server of its own with a socket in \fI/var/run/otpme/sockets/\fR, and OTPme starts it and writes its configuration.
Objects and search results stay in the memory of each process, see \fBOBJECT_CACHES\fR; sessions are files like the objects.
.TP
.B CACHE
The cache server: \fBredis\fR (default) or \fBmemcached\fR.
Run \fBotpme\-tool cache stop\fR before changing it.
.TP
.B AUTOSTART_CACHE
Whether \fBotpme\-controld\fR starts the cache server when it starts, and stops it when it stops (default: \fBTrue\fR).
With \fBFalse\fR the server has to run already, else the daemons do not start: "Cache not started".
The global option \fB\-\-no\-cache\-start\fR does the same for one start.
.TP
.B FLUSH_CACHE_ON_START
Whether \fBotpme\-controld\fR empties the cache when it starts (default: \fBTrue\fR).
With \fBFalse\fR the global option \fB\-\-cache\-flush\fR does it for one start.
.TP
.B OBJECT_CACHES
How many objects of a type each process keeps in its own memory, as \fItype\fR\fB:\fR\fIcount\fR pairs separated by commas, e.g. \fBuser:2048,group:512\fR.
When a type is full, the quarter used longest ago goes.
The defaults: \fBuser\fR and \fBtoken\fR 10240, \fBscript\fR 512, \fBdictionary\fR 128, every other type 1024.
A type not named keeps its default.
.SS Redis
.TP
.B REDIS_SERVER_BIN\fR, \fBREDIS_CLI_BIN
The Redis programs (default: \fBredis\-server\fR and \fBredis\-cli\fR, found in \fBPATH\fR).
\fBotpme\-tool cache cli\fR opens \fBredis\-cli\fR.
.TP
.B REDIS_SOCKET
The socket (default: \fI/var/run/otpme/sockets/redis.sock\fR).
Redis listens on no TCP port.
.TP
.B REDIS_MAXMEMORY
How much memory Redis may use (default: \fB128M\fR).
.TP
.B REDIS_MAXMEMORY_POLICY
What goes when that is full: \fBlru\fR, the entries used longest ago (Redis \fBvolatile\-lru\fR), or \fBlfu\fR, the ones used least often (\fBvolatile\-lfu\fR) (default: \fBlru\fR).
.TP
.B REDIS_MAXMEMORY_SAMPLES
With \fBlru\fR: how many entries Redis looks at to pick one (default: \fB5\fR).
.TP
.B REDIS_LFU_LOG_FACTOR\fR, \fBREDIS_LFU_DECAY_TIME
With \fBlfu\fR: Redis \fBlfu\-log\-factor\fR and \fBlfu\-decay\-time\fR (default: \fB10\fR and \fB1\fR).
.TP
.B REDIS_LOGLEVEL
The log level of Redis, written to \fI/var/log/otpme/redis.log\fR (default: \fBnotice\fR).
.TP
.B REDIS_DATABASES
How many databases Redis has (default: \fB16\fR).
.TP
.B REDIS_PERSISTENCE
Whether Redis writes the cache to disk, in \fBREDIS_CACHE_DIR\fR (default: \fBFalse\fR).
The cache holds password hashes; with \fBTrue\fR they are on the disk.
With \fBFalse\fR OTPme also turns it off on a running Redis.
.TP
.B REDIS_CACHE_DIR
Where Redis writes with \fBREDIS_PERSISTENCE\fR (default: \fI/var/cache/otpme/redis\fR).
.PP
A file \fI/etc/otpme/redis.conf\fR is used instead of the one OTPme writes.
Then none of the settings above apply, but \fBREDIS_PERSISTENCE\fR still does.
.SS Memcached
.TP
.B MEMCACHED_BIN
The memcached program (default: \fBmemcached\fR, found in \fBPATH\fR).
.TP
.B MEMCACHED_MAXMEM
How much memory memcached may use, in MB (default: \fB128\fR).
.TP
.B MEMCACHED_THREADS
The threads of memcached (default: \fB32\fR).
.TP
.B MEMCACHED_MAXCONN
How many connections memcached takes (default: \fB4096\fR).
The default of memcached itself, 1024, is not enough here: the connection pool holds one connection per thread and process and never gives it back, so every daemon, every ldapd worker and every forked child adds to the count.
Once the limit is reached memcached turns new connections away, which reaches the client as a connection reset on a cache that is running fine:
.RS
.PP
.B Memcache get error: ... CONNECTION FAILURE(Connection reset by peer)
.RE
.TP
.B MEMCACHED_MAX_OBJECT_SIZE
The largest entry memcached takes (default: \fB8m\fR).
Make it larger if roles or groups with many members grow beyond it.
.TP
.B MEMCACHED_SOCKET\fR, \fBMEMCACHED_PIDFILE
The socket and the PID file (default: \fI/var/run/otpme/sockets/memcached.sock\fR and \fI/var/run/otpme/pidfiles/memcached.pid\fR).
Memcached listens on no TCP or UDP port.
.TP
.B MEMCACHED_OPTS
The whole command line of memcached.
All other memcached settings above are then ignored, so it has to give \fB\-s\fR with the socket and \fB\-P\fR with the PID file: OTPme looks for memcached there.
.hy
.ad
.SH FREERADIUS
.na
.nh
On a node OTPme runs a FreeRADIUS server of its own on port 1812, with its configuration in \fI/var/run/otpme/freeradius/\fR.
It runs only while the node is enabled and the cluster has a quorum; otherwise it is stopped.
The RADIUS clients are the OTPme clients, see
.BR otpme\-client (1).
.PP
\fBotpme\-controld\fR starts it as root, and FreeRADIUS then changes to its own user.
With the module (\fBUSE_RADIUS_MOD\fR) that is \fBFREERADIUS_USER\fR: it has no access to the objects of OTPme and gets the client of a request and the session of a cached login from \fBotpme\-hostd\fR.
Without the module it is the OTPme user, because \fBotpme\-auth\fR reads the auth cache of the node.
The configuration directory and its files belong to the OTPme user and to the group of FreeRADIUS, which may only read them.
.TP
.B START_FREERADIUS
Whether the node runs FreeRADIUS (default: \fBTrue\fR).
.TP
.B FREERADIUS_BIN
The FreeRADIUS program (default: \fI/usr/sbin/freeradius\fR).
.TP
.B FREERADIUS_USER\fR, \fBFREERADIUS_GROUP
The user and group FreeRADIUS runs as with the module (default: \fBfreerad\fR and \fBfreerad\fR).
.TP
.B FREERADIUS_LOG_DIR
Where FreeRADIUS writes \fIradius.log\fR with the module, and the module its audit log \fIaudit.log\fR (default: \fI/var/log/freeradius\fR).
The module does not send its audit log to \fBAUDIT_LOG_SERVER\fR, because \fBFREERADIUS_USER\fR may not read the certificate and key of the node; what authd does for it goes there as always.
It is made for \fBFREERADIUS_USER\fR when it is missing.
Without the module \fIradius.log\fR is in the log directory of OTPme.
.TP
.B USE_RADIUS_MOD
Whether FreeRADIUS checks a request through the Python module of OTPme (default: \fBTrue\fR).
The module also adds the VLAN and the MPPE keys to the answer.
With \fBFalse\fR every request starts \fBotpme\-auth verify\fR, which is slower, and no VLAN is sent.
Either way an answer comes from the cache only where the client has its auth cache enabled, for its \fBauth_cache_timeout\fR, see \fBTHE AUTH CACHE\fR in
.BR otpme\-client (1).
.TP
.B RADIUS_START_SERVERS\fR, \fBRADIUS_MAX_SERVERS\fR, \fBRADIUS_MIN_SPARE_SERVERS\fR, \fBRADIUS_MAX_SPARE_SERVERS
The thread pool of FreeRADIUS: \fBstart_servers\fR, \fBmax_servers\fR, \fBmin_spare_servers\fR and \fBmax_spare_servers\fR (default: \fB32\fR, \fB64\fR, \fB8\fR and \fB16\fR).
.PP
A file \fI/etc/otpme/freeradius/radiusd.conf\fR is used instead of the one OTPme writes.
Then none of the settings above apply but \fBSTART_FREERADIUS\fR, \fBFREERADIUS_BIN\fR and \fBFREERADIUS_GROUP\fR, and the user FreeRADIUS runs as is the one the file gives.
The log of the module is \fBRADIUS_MOD_LOGFILE\fR, see \fBPAM LOGGING\fR.
.hy
.ad
.SH HAPROXY (LDAP LOAD BALANCING)
.na
.nh
An haproxy on the floating IP of the site spreads LDAP connections over the ldapd of all enabled member nodes, its own one included, to the one with the fewest connections.
It runs on the node that holds the floating IP, started and stopped by \fBotpme\-controld\fR along with it, so it follows the master to another node.
When a member node comes or goes it is reloaded without dropping a connection.
.PP
It passes the connections on as they are, LDAPS with the site certificate, so the \fBdc=\fIclient\fR of a bind DN still picks the access group.
ldapd sees the address of haproxy, not that of the client.
Clients can still reach every ldapd directly on its own port, 2026.
.PP
The nodes are reached by their FQDN, so they have to resolve.
Without a site address haproxy is not started.
.TP
.B START_HAPROXY
Whether the node runs haproxy (default: \fBFalse\fR; the distributed \fIotpme.conf\fR sets \fBTrue\fR).
haproxy has to be installed.
.TP
.B HAPROXY_BIN
The haproxy program (default: \fI/usr/sbin/haproxy\fR).
.TP
.B HAPROXY_PORT
The port on the floating IP that clients connect to (default: \fB636\fR).
636 is where LDAP clients look for LDAPS, which is what dovecot and SOGo want to be pointed at.
.TP
.B HAPROXY_MAXCONN
How many connections haproxy takes at once (default: \fB4096\fR).
.TP
.B HAPROXY_CHECK_INTERVAL
How often each node is checked with a TCP connect (default: \fB5s\fR).
A node is out after 3 failed checks and back after 2 good ones.
.TP
.B HAPROXY_CONNECT_TIMEOUT
How long haproxy waits for a connection to an ldapd (default: \fB5s\fR).
.TP
.B HAPROXY_CLIENT_TIMEOUT\fR, \fBHAPROXY_SERVER_TIMEOUT
How long an idle connection to the client and to the ldapd is kept (default: \fB1h\fR and \fB1h\fR).
Generous on purpose: dovecot keeps its LDAP connection open between logins, and cutting an idle one only makes it reconnect.
.PP
The times are in the format of haproxy, e.g. \fB5s\fR, \fB10m\fR, \fB1h\fR.
.PP
A file \fI/etc/otpme/haproxy/haproxy.cfg\fR is used instead of the one OTPme writes.
Then none of the settings above apply but \fBSTART_HAPROXY\fR and \fBHAPROXY_BIN\fR, and the member nodes are not added: the file has to name them itself.
.hy
.ad
.SH ENCRYPTION
.na
.nh
On a node and on a host the object files are encrypted with the master key, a Fernet key (AES with HMAC) of 44 base64 characters.
.TP
.B KEY_COMMAND
Where the master key comes from (default: \fBfile://etc/otpme/otpme.key\fR).
.RS
.TP
.B file://\fIpath\fR
A file that holds the key.
\fIpath\fR is the absolute path without its first slash: \fBfile://etc/otpme/otpme.key\fR is \fI/etc/otpme/otpme.key\fR.
\fBotpme\-realm init\fR and the join of a host or node write it, from a master password that is asked for, and keep its salt in \fI/etc/otpme/otpme.key_salt\fR.
\fBotpme\-tool regen_master_key\fR writes it again from the password and that salt.
.TP
.I command
A program, with its arguments, that writes the key to stdout.
OTPme then writes no key file; the program has to give the same key every time.
.RE
.TP
.B MASTER_KEY_HASH_TYPE
How the master key is made from the master password (default: \fBArgon2_i\fR).
The hash type is not kept with the salt: change it after the key was made and \fBregen_master_key\fR gives another key.
.TP
.B OFFLINE_TOKEN_HASH_TYPE
How the key that encrypts the offline login data of a user on a host is made from their password (default: \fBArgon2_i\fR).
It counts for offline logins saved from then on.
A user can set it in \fI~/.otpme/otpme.conf\fR as well.
.TP
.B OBJECT_EXPORT_HASH_TYPE
How \fBexport \-\-password\fR makes its key from the password (default: \fBArgon2_i\fR).
The export keeps the salt but not the hash type: \fBotpme\-tool import\fR has to run with the same one.
A user can set it in \fI~/.otpme/otpme.conf\fR as well.
.PP
The hash types are \fBArgon2_i\fR, \fBArgon2_id\fR, \fBArgon2_d\fR and \fBPBKDF2\fR.
.hy
.ad
.SH SSL
.na
.nh
The files a host or node gets when it joins the realm, and that hostd keeps up to date.
OTPme writes them; change the paths only to have them somewhere else.
.TP
.B SSL_CERT_FILE\fR, \fBSSL_KEY_FILE
The certificate of this host or node, issued by the CA of its site, and its private key (default: \fI/etc/otpme/ssl/cert.pem\fR and \fI/etc/otpme/ssl/key.pem\fR).
Daemons answer with them, and commands connect with them, also the ones a user starts: so both are owned by \fBotpme\fR and readable by the group \fBrealmusers\fR, mode 0640.
.TP
.B SSL_CA_FILE
The CA certificates a host or node trusts (default: \fI/etc/otpme/ssl/ca.pem\fR), mode 0640 like the two above.
.TP
.B SSL_SITE_CERT_FILE
The certificate of each site, one file per site (default: \fI/etc/otpme/ssl/{realm}\-{site}.pem\fR).
\fB{realm}\fR and \fB{site}\fR stand for the names: \fI/etc/otpme/ssl/example.org\-berlin.pem\fR.
A file that is there is kept as it is when a sync brings another certificate; \fBotpme\-tool sync \-\-request\-site\-cert\fR replaces it, see
.BR otpme\-tool (1).
Mode 0644.
.TP
.B HOST_KEY_FILE
The RSA key a host or node authenticates itself with (default: \fI/etc/otpme/ssl/hostkey.pem\fR).
Mode 0600.
.hy
.ad
.SH DIRECTORIES
.na
.nh
Where OTPme keeps its files.
A change takes effect when the daemons start again; the files that are there are not moved.
.TP
.B DATA_DIR
The objects and what goes with them (default: \fI/var/lib/otpme\fR): the object files in \fItree/\fR, the index in \fIindex/\fR, the sessions, the trash and the data objects such as jobs and inventories in \fIdata/\fR.
.TP
.B SPOOL_DIR
Work in progress (default: \fI/var/spool/otpme\fR): open transactions, the sync, the cluster journal and what is waiting for nsscache.
.TP
.B CACHE_DIR
What a host keeps for itself (default: \fI/var/cache/otpme\fR): the offline logins, the nsscache files, the keys of signers, the Redis files with \fBREDIS_PERSISTENCE\fR.
.TP
.B RUN_DIR
PID files, sockets and locks, and the configuration OTPme writes for FreeRADIUS and haproxy (default: \fI/var/run/otpme\fR).
.TP
.B LOG_DIR
The log files (default: \fI/var/log/otpme\fR).
.TP
.B MOUNT_ROOT_DIR
Where shares are mounted on a host (default: \fI/otpme\fR), as \fI<user>/<realm>/<site>/<share>\fR.
It is owned by root and the group \fBrealmusers\fR, mode 0770.
.TP
.B SHARE_ROOT
Where the shares of a node keep their data, for every share on this node.
Set, it counts instead of the config parameter \fBshare_root\fR of the shares, see
.BR otpme\-share (1).
.hy
.ad
.SH DNS
.na
.nh
OTPme reaches the daemons of a site by the FQDNs of the site (\fBauth_fqdn\fR, \fBmgmt_fqdn\fR, see
.BR otpme\-site (1)),
so those have to resolve; without them it takes the address of the site.
Beyond that DNS says where to log in and where to join, from these records in the DNS domain of the host:
.PP
.RS
.nf
_otpme\-realm._tcp   TXT  "example.org"
_otpme\-site._tcp    TXT  "berlin"
_otpme\-join._tcp    SRV  10 1 2024 login.example.org.
.fi
.RE
.PP
The domain is the FQDN of the host without its first label: \fBnb1.example.org\fR looks in \fBexample.org\fR.
.TP
.B LOGIN_USE_DNS
Whether a login on a host goes to the realm and site the TXT records name, instead of those the host belongs to (default: \fBTrue\fR).
That lets a notebook of one site log in at another.
A node never asks.
The global options \fB\-\-login\-use\-dns\fR and \fB\-\-login\-no\-dns\fR override it for one command.
.PP
\fBotpme\-tool join\fR always asks DNS, in the domain it is given, else in \fIsite\fB.\fIrealm\fR, else in the domain of the host: the SRV record for joind (unless \fB\-\-joind\-uri\fR names it), else the address of the domain itself with port 2024, and the TXT records for the realm and site it is not given.
.hy
.ad
.SH LOGIN
.na
.nh
.TP
.B VALID_LOGIN_USERS\fR, \fBDENY_LOGIN_USERS
Which users may log in on this host, as names separated by commas (default: empty, everybody).
An entry \fIname\fB:\fIuuid\fR also matches the user with that UUID, so a renamed user stays matched.
A user in \fBDENY_LOGIN_USERS\fR is refused, also when \fBVALID_LOGIN_USERS\fR names them; with \fBVALID_LOGIN_USERS\fR set, everybody else is refused.
It counts for a login through pam_otpme, and the SSH keys of a refused user are not written to their \fIauthorized_keys\fR.
.TP
.B ALLOW_DYNAMIC_GROUPS\fR, \fBDENY_DYNAMIC_GROUPS
Which dynamic groups a user gets at login, as names separated by commas (default: empty, all).
A dynamic group is a group of this host that pam_otpme adds the user to: those of the host and those of the login token and its roles, see \fBadd_dynamic_group\fR in
.BR otpme\-role (1).
A group in \fBDENY_DYNAMIC_GROUPS\fR is left out; with \fBALLOW_DYNAMIC_GROUPS\fR set, only the groups it names are added.
\fBALLOW_DYNAMIC_GROUPS\fR \fBFalse\fR adds none.
A group that does not exist on the host is left out with a warning.
.hy
.ad
.SH SIGNERS
.na
.nh
A signer is a user or a role whose signatures this host accepts, see \fBadd_signer\fR in
.BR otpme\-tool (1).
The signers of the host check token signatures (e.g. whether an SSH token is signed for a group), the key script and the agent script.
A user can have private signers of their own for these three, in \fI~/.otpme/signers/\fR (\fBadd_signer \-\-private\fR).
These parameters say whether those count.
A disabled signer never counts.
.TP
.B FORCE_TOKEN_SIGNERS\fR, \fBFORCE_KEY_SCRIPT_SIGNERS\fR, \fBFORCE_AGENT_SCRIPT_SIGNERS
For token signatures, the key script and the agent script (default: \fBTrue\fR):
.RS
.TP
.B True
Only the signers of the host count; private signers are ignored.
.TP
.B False
A user who has private signers gets them instead of those of the host.
.TP
.I user\fR[\fB,\fIuser\fR]...
Only the signers of the host count for these users; every other user gets their private signers, where they have any.
.RE
.hy
.ad
.SH SCRIPTS
.na
.nh
scriptd runs the scripts of a node: authorization scripts of users and tokens, the scripts of \fBscript_otp\fR and \fBscript_static\fR tokens, push scripts, share scripts and the vote script.
It never runs a script as root or as \fBUSER\fR/\fBGROUP\fR.
An authorization script, the script of a \fBscript_otp\fR or \fBscript_static\fR token and a push script run as the user they are for and with the group of that user.
The authorization script of a user and a push script also get the other groups of the user.
A share script and the vote script run as the user and group below.
.TP
.B ROOT_SCRIPT_USER\fR, \fBROOT_SCRIPT_GROUP
The user and group a script runs as instead of root or \fBUSER\fR/\fBGROUP\fR (default: \fBnobody\fR and \fBnogroup\fR).
.hy
.ad
.SH PAM LOGGING
.na
.nh
Where pam_otpme writes its log, see
.BR pam_otpme (8).
It uses the first of \fBPAM_USE_LOGFILE\fR, \fBPAM_USE_SYSLOG\fR and \fBPAM_USE_SYSTEMD\fR that is \fBTrue\fR.
If none is, it logs nothing.
The level is \fBLOGLEVEL\fR.
.TP
.B PAM_USE_LOGFILE
Write to \fBPAM_LOGFILE\fR (default: \fBFalse\fR).
A screen unlock runs pam_otpme as the user, who can not write to that file; pam_otpme then uses the next one that is \fBTrue\fR.
.TP
.B PAM_LOGFILE
The log file (default: \fI/var/log/otpme/pam.log\fR).
.TP
.B PAM_USE_SYSLOG
Send to the local syslog (\fI/dev/log\fR), with facility \fBLOCAL7\fR (default: \fBFalse\fR).
.TP
.B PAM_USE_SYSTEMD
Send to the systemd journal (default: \fBTrue\fR).
.TP
.B RADIUS_MOD_LOGFILE
The log file of the FreeRADIUS module of OTPme, see \fBFREERADIUS\fR (default: \fI/var/log/freeradius/radius\-module.log\fR).
It has to be a file \fBFREERADIUS_USER\fR may write.
The module always writes there.
.hy
.ad
.SH AUDIT LOG
.na
.nh
Every daemon of OTPme writes an audit log: each change of an object (who did it, from which client, in which session, and what), and the logins, sessions and other events of its protocols.
The agent writes none.
.PP
Without \fBAUDIT_LOG_ENABLED\fR and \fBAUDIT_LOG_SERVER\fR the audit log goes to \fIaudit.log\fR in \fBLOG_DIR\fR.
With both, it goes to that syslog server instead.
An entry the server does not get is kept in \fIaudit_log/\fR in \fBSPOOL_DIR\fR, and hostd sends it again every 60 seconds, starting with \fBLOG_RESEND:\fR and the time it was written.
.TP
.B AUDIT_LOG_ENABLED
Send the audit log to \fBAUDIT_LOG_SERVER\fR (default: \fBFalse\fR).
.TP
.B AUDIT_LOG_SERVER
The syslog server, as \fIhost\fB:\fIport\fR for TCP, or as \fIhost\fR for UDP on port 514 (default: none).
TLS and RELP need the port.
.TP
.B AUDIT_LOG_PROTOCOL
\fBsyslog\fR (default) or \fBrelp\fR.
.TP
.B AUDIT_LOG_FACILITY
The syslog facility, e.g. \fBDAEMON\fR, \fBAUTH\fR or \fBLOCAL0\fR to \fBLOCAL7\fR (default: \fBDAEMON\fR).
.TP
.B AUDIT_LOG_USE_TLS
Talk TLS to the server (default: \fBFalse\fR).
Its certificate must be signed by \fBAUDIT_LOG_CA_CERT\fR and hold the host name of \fBAUDIT_LOG_SERVER\fR.
Without \fBAUDIT_LOG_CA_CERT\fR the audit log goes to the file.
.TP
.B AUDIT_LOG_CA_CERT
The CA certificate that checks the server (default: none).
.TP
.B AUDIT_LOG_USE_CLIENT_CERT
With TLS, also show the server a certificate of this host: \fBAUDIT_LOG_CERT\fR and \fBAUDIT_LOG_KEY\fR (default: \fBFalse\fR).
Without them the audit log goes to the file.
.TP
.BR AUDIT_LOG_CERT ", " AUDIT_LOG_KEY
The client certificate and its key (default: none).
.hy
.ad
.SH SYSLOG
.na
.nh
With \fBSYSLOG_ENABLED\fR and \fBSYSLOG_SERVER\fR the daemons send their log to that syslog server instead of their log files in \fBLOG_DIR\fR.
A daemon started with \fB\-d\fR, and \fBotpme\-realm init\fR, still log to the terminal.
A message the server does not get is lost: unlike the audit log it is not kept.
Without \fBSYSLOG_CA_CERT\fR, or without \fBSYSLOG_CERT\fR or \fBSYSLOG_KEY\fR where they are needed, the daemons log to their files.
The parameters work like those of \fBAUDIT LOG\fR:
.TP
.B SYSLOG_ENABLED
Send the log to \fBSYSLOG_SERVER\fR (default: \fBFalse\fR).
.TP
.B SYSLOG_SERVER
\fIhost\fB:\fIport\fR for TCP, or \fIhost\fR for UDP on port 514 (default: none).
TLS and RELP need the port.
.TP
.B SYSLOG_PROTOCOL
\fBsyslog\fR (default) or \fBrelp\fR.
.TP
.B SYSLOG_FACILITY
The syslog facility (default: \fBDAEMON\fR).
.TP
.B SYSLOG_USE_TLS
Talk TLS to the server, checked with \fBSYSLOG_CA_CERT\fR (default: \fBFalse\fR).
.TP
.B SYSLOG_CA_CERT
The CA certificate that checks the server (default: none).
.TP
.B SYSLOG_USE_CLIENT_CERT
With TLS, also show the server \fBSYSLOG_CERT\fR and \fBSYSLOG_KEY\fR (default: \fBFalse\fR).
.TP
.BR SYSLOG_CERT ", " SYSLOG_KEY
The client certificate and its key (default: none).
.hy
.ad
.SH SYNCHRONIZATION
.na
.nh
hostd syncs the objects of this host, and its other data, from the master node of its site.
On the master node, hostd syncs from the master nodes of the other sites.
It also starts a sync when it is told to, see \fBsync\fR in
.BR otpme\-tool (1).
.TP
.B SYNC_INTERVAL
Seconds from one sync to the next (default: \fB180\fR).
.TP
.B SYNC_RETRY_INTERVAL
Seconds until a sync that failed is tried again (default: \fB10\fR).
.TP
.B SYNC_RETRY_COUNT
How many runs one sync may take to reach the state of the master node (default: \fB5\fR).
An object that changes on the master node while it is fetched makes another run.
After the last one the sync counts as failed.
.TP
.B SYNC_IGNORE_CHANGED_OBJECTS
Keep an object as it came, even if it changed on the master node while it was fetched, and make no further run for it (default: \fBFalse\fR).
This helps when objects change faster than a sync can follow; the objects here can then be out of step with each other until the next sync.
For one sync, use \fBdo_sync \-\-ignore\-changed\-objects\fR in
.BR otpme\-tool (1).
.TP
.B PIN_HOST_KEYS
If True, write down the public key of every host that is synced here, and hold the host to it.
.RS
.PP
A remote access session is checked against the key a host was joined with, read from this host's copy of the tree.
Whoever can change the tree can change that key.
With this, they cannot change it here: the key written down is the one a session is checked against.
.PP
The first key of a host is written down by the sync that brings it \(em it comes from the realm, not from the host, so there is nobody to ask.
A key that differs from the one written down does not replace it.
The sync says so in its log, and you take the new key over by hand as root:
.PP
.RS
.B otpme\-tool update_host_keys
.RE
.PP
Until then a session to that host does not open.
A host that was never synced here has no key written down and gets no session either.
.PP
The sync never removes a key it wrote down, not even the key of a host that is gone.
Else whoever controls a node could delete a host and add it again, with the same UUID and a key of their own.
.B otpme\-tool update_host_keys
removes the keys of hosts that are gone.
.PP
Without it a host gets only its own host object, and the copies of the other hosts it had are removed.
So a remote access session from a host to another host needs this.
A session to a node works either way, because a host gets all nodes.
.PP
Default False, because it means a command for every host whose key changes.
See
.BR otpme\-shell (1)
and
.BR otpme\-tool (1).
.RE
.TP
.B PIN_USER_KEYS
If True, write down the encrypt public key of every user that is synced here, and hold the user to it.
Only on hosts.
.RS
.PP
.B otpme\-pass
encrypts the key of a password for every member, with the encrypt public key of that member.
That key comes from the nodes.
Whoever runs a node could hand in a key of their own for a member, and read every password that is written after that.
With this, the key from the nodes must be the one written down here, or the member gets no key from this host.
.PP
It works like
.BR PIN_HOST_KEYS :
the sync writes down the first key of a user, a key that differs does not replace it, and you take the new key over by hand as root:
.PP
.RS
.B otpme\-tool update_user_keys
.RE
.PP
The sync never removes a key it wrote down, not even the key of a user that is gone.
.B update_user_keys
removes those as well.
.PP
A member whose user this host does not get has no key written down here, and gets no key from this host.
.PP
Default False.
See
.BR otpme\-pass (1).
.RE
.hy
.ad
.SH DAEMON
.na
.nh
.TP
.B MASTER_FAILOVER_SYNC
Sync once more before the master role moves on request, see \fBmaster_failover\fR in
.BR otpme\-cluster (1)
(default: \fBFalse\fR).
The node that takes the role syncs the data objects, the sessions and then the objects from the old master node.
A sync that fails is tried again every second, until it works.
.TP
.B TWO_NODE_TIMEOUT
Seconds a node of a two\-node site waits for the other node when clusterd starts (default: \fB30\fR).
If the other node does not come in that time, the node elects a master node alone.
See \fBTWO NODES\fR in
.BR otpme\-cluster (1).
.TP
.B CONTROLD_HEARTBEAT_INTERVAL
Seconds from one check of the daemons to the next (default: \fB15\fR).
At each check controld sends a heartbeat to each of its daemons, and starts one that is not running.
.TP
.B CONTROLD_HEARTBEAT_TIMEOUT
Seconds controld waits for the answer to a heartbeat (default: \fB15\fR).
If you see \(dqDaemon '<name>' is not responding\(dq in the log under high load, make this longer.
.TP
.B CONTROLD_HEARTBEAT_RETRY_INTERVAL
Seconds until controld sends a heartbeat again that got no answer (default: \fB3\fR).
.TP
.B CONTROLD_HEARTBEAT_RETRY
How many heartbeats in a row may go without an answer (default: \fB3\fR).
After the last one, controld restarts the daemon.
.TP
.B BACKEND_POLICY_INTERVAL
Seconds between two runs of the policies of an object when it is read, for example
.B auto_disable
(default: \fB60\fR).
The policies run when the object is read and this time has passed since their last run, in each process on its own.
So an object can stay enabled up to this long after its time to disable has come.
A short time means more work on each read.
.hy
.ad
.SH AGENT
.na
.nh
otpme\-agent runs as the user, but it reads these from this file only, not from the config file of the user.
It holds a connection to each daemon a login session uses, for example authd or mgmtd.
.TP
.B AGENT_CONNECTION_IDLE_TIMEOUT
Seconds after the last request on a daemon connection until otpme\-agent closes it (default: \fB300\fR).
Keepalive messages do not count as requests.
The next request opens the connection again.
.TP
.B AGENT_KEEPALIVE_INTERVAL
Seconds without a message on a daemon connection until otpme\-agent sends a keepalive message on it (default: \fB5\fR).
A connection whose keepalive message fails is closed.
This is also the longest time otpme\-agent waits between two checks of its login sessions.
.TP
.B AGENT_PASS_CACHE_INTERVAL
Seconds from one fetch of the passwords this host may keep for offline use to the next (default: \fB3600\fR).
otpme\-agent also fetches them at the first login of the user, and when one of them changes.
Which passwords a host may keep is set with the config parameter
.B password_offline_cache
of a unit or site.
.TP
.B AGENT_CONFIRM_ACTIONS
Whether otpme\-agent asks to confirm each use of a YubiKey in PIV mode, signing or decrypting, while it holds the PIN login of the card (default: \fBTrue\fR).
It shows what the use is for, e.g. the share or the password.
Without the PIN login it asks for the PIN instead, with the same text.
.br
It asks only in a graphical session of the user: on a console the shell reads the terminal, so the card is used without asking there.
.br
The user cannot switch it off.
.hy
.ad
.SH NETWORK
.na
.nh
.TP
.B MAX_REQUEST_SIZE
The largest single request OTPme accepts from the other side, in bytes (default: \fB104857600\fR, 100 MiB).
A request larger than this is refused.
This is a safety limit and not a speed setting.
Raise it only when a backup, a sync or a restore fails because something you move is bigger than it.
Most daemons have a smaller limit of their own; this value is used where a daemon has none.
.TP
.B POSIX_MSGSIZE_MAX
The largest message, in bytes, in the POSIX message queues the OTPme processes talk over (default: \fB8192\fR).
A longer message is sent in parts.
\fBauto\fR reads it from
.IR /proc/sys/fs/mqueue/msgsize_max .
Linux refuses a larger value than that file for a process without the privilege to exceed it, see
.BR mq_overview (7).
.TP
.B RLIMIT_MSGQUEUE
The most bytes all POSIX message queues of a user may hold together (default: \fB2621440000\fR), see
.BR getrlimit (2).
Applied when an OTPme process starts.
As with \fBRLIMIT_NOFILE\fR, it is only ever raised, never lowered, and only root can raise the hard limit.
A process without privileges takes the soft limit only as high as the hard limit allows.
.TP
.B RLIMIT_NOFILE
The most files a process may have open (default: \fB65536\fR), see
.BR getrlimit (2).
Applied when an OTPme process starts.
.br
Set here rather than left to the system, because
.I /etc/security/limits.conf
does not reach a daemon: that file is read by
.BR pam_limits (8),
which only runs for a PAM session \- a login, an ssh connection, su.
A service started at boot never passes through it and keeps the kernel default of 1024.
With a socket per connection across the daemons under
.BR otpme\-controld ,
plus cluster, redis and the index database, that runs out and shows up as \(dqtoo many open files\(dq.
.br
Only ever raised, never lowered, so a higher limit set through your init system (\fBLimitNOFILE\fR for systemd, \fBrc_ulimit\fR in \fI/etc/conf.d/otpme\fR for OpenRC) is left alone.
Only root can raise the hard limit.
A process without privileges, such as a command or the otpme\-agent of a user, takes the soft limit only as high as the hard limit allows.
.TP
.B FLOATING_IP_IFACE
The network interface the floating IP of the site is put on, on the master node, e.g. \fBeth0\fR (default: not set).
Without it, the interface whose network holds the address is taken, with the netmask of that network.
With it, the address is put on this interface as a single host (/32, or /128 for IPv6).
.hy
.ad
.SH STORAGE
.na
.nh
.TP
.B OBJECT_JSON_COMPRESSION
How the files of the objects are compressed: \fBlz4\fR, \fBgzip\fR, \fBzlib\fR or \fBbzip\fR (default: not set, uncompressed; \fBnone\fR is the same).
A file is read with whatever it was written with, so a change takes effect with the next write of each object, and the old files stay readable.
Any other value is refused when OTPme starts.
.TP
.B PRETTIFY_OBJECT_JSON
Write the files of the objects with line breaks and indentation, easier to read and bigger (default: \fBFalse\fR).
.TP
.B FSYNC_FILE_WRITES
Sync every file OTPme writes to disk before it goes on: objects, transactions, keys (default: \fBFalse\fR).
.br
A file is written under another name first and then put in place of the old one, so a crash leaves the old file or the new one, never part of one.
(Not a file with a second hard link, or one OTPme may write but not replace: those are written in place.)
On ext4 that holds without this, and a crash loses only what was written in the last seconds.
Turn it on for another filesystem, or where those seconds must not be lost either.
.br
It costs a few milliseconds on every file, and a change of one object writes several of them.
Measure it on your disks before you turn it on for a site with many changes.
.hy
.ad
.SH LOGGING
.na
.nh
.TP
.B LOGLEVEL
The lowest level that is logged: \fBCRITICAL\fR, \fBERROR\fR, \fBWARNING\fR, \fBINFO\fR or \fBDEBUG\fR (default: \fBINFO\fR).
Any other value is taken as \fBINFO\fR, with a message.
Debug options such as \fB\-dd\fR log \fBDEBUG\fR whatever is set here.
.TP
.B LOG_AUTH_DATA
Print what each authentication request sends: password, OTP, SOTP, challenge and so on (default: \fBFalse\fR).
\fBTrue\fR does it for every user, a comma separated list of user names for these users only.
It works only with \fBLOGLEVEL\fR \fBDEBUG\fR or a debug option.
It prints to the standard output of the daemon, never to the log file or syslog: in the foreground that is the terminal, under systemd the journal.
Use it only to find a fault in a test setup, never in production.
.TP
.B LOG_EXC_INFO
Add the traceback of the exception that is being handled to warning, error and critical log messages (default: \fBFalse\fR).
Only for messages that are written while an exception is handled; where the code says itself whether to add it, that wins.
.TP
.B TRACEBACKS
Show the Python traceback when a command or daemon ends with an error (default: \fBFalse\fR).
Without it, only the error message is shown.
Read when the process starts.
.TP
.B SHOW_JOB_TITLE
Show the name of the job in the process title of a job, after its UUID (default: \fBFalse\fR).
.hy
.ad
.SH SSO
.na
.nh
.TP
.B SSO_SERVER
Run the SSO portal, \fBhttpd\fR, on this host (default: \fBFalse\fR).
Only for hosts: a node runs it always, whatever is set here.
.br
The host has to be an SSO host of its site, see \fBadd_sso_host\fR in
.BR otpme\-site (1).
Only then is it synced what the portal needs: \fBSSO_CERT\fR, \fBSSO_KEY\fR, \fBSSO_SECRET\fR and \fBSSO_CSRF_SECRET\fR.
Without that, httpd stops at start with an error that says so.
.hy
.ad
.SH BACKUP
.na
.nh
.SS Backup server
These make this system a backup server.
.TP
.B BACKUP_SERVER
Accept backups from other systems (default: \fBFalse\fR).
On a node, backupd always runs, for the backups of the node and its shares, and this makes it listen for others as well.
On a host, this starts backupd.
.TP
.B BACKUP_DIR
The directory all repositories are kept in (default: \fI/var/backups\fR).
A repository \fItype\fR/\fIsite\fR/\fIname\fR is the directory \fIsite\fR/\fItype\fR/\fIname\fR in it.
It has to exist.
.TP
.B ALLOW_NEW_BACKUP_REPOS
Who may create a new repository with their first backup (default: \fBFalse\fR).
\fBFalse\fR: nobody, \fBTrue\fR: everyone, or a comma separated list of the names (CN) in the client certificates that may.
A repository that exists asks for its password either way.
.SS Backup of this system
The backups of nodes and shares are set with config parameters of these objects, see
.BR otpme\-backup (1).
The ones below are for the backup of a host, or of a system that is not part of the realm.
They are used by \fBotpme\-backup start localhost\fR, as root.
Nothing starts it on a schedule; run it from cron or a systemd timer.
.TP
.B BACKUP_REPOSITORY
Where the backup goes: \fIserver\fR:\fItype\fR/\fIsite\fR/\fIname\fR, e.g. \fBnode1.berlin.example.org:host/berlin/client1\fR (default: not set).
\fIserver\fR is the address of a backup server.
.TP
.B BACKUP_MODE
How a new repository stores its files: \fBpack\fR or \fBtree\fR (default: \fBpack\fR).
It counts only when the repository is created; after that it keeps its mode.
See
.BR otpme\-backup (1).
.TP
.B BACKUP_REPO_PASS_FILE
The file with the password of the repository, in its first line (default: \fI/etc/otpme/backup.pass\fR).
.TP
.B BACKUP_KEY_FILE
The file with the key the backup is encrypted with, as 64 hex digits in its first line (default: \fI/etc/otpme/backup.key\fR).
Make one with:
.RS
.PP
.B openssl rand \-hex 32 > /etc/otpme/backup.key
.RE
.IP
Keep a copy elsewhere: without the key, nothing in the backup can be read.
.IP
Both files are read only by root, and not at all if anyone may read them: then OTPme says so.
.TP
.B BACKUP_EXCLUDE_SPECIAL
Leave out special files: devices, sockets, pipes (default: \fBTrue\fR).
.TP
.B BACKUP_EXCLUDES
A comma separated list of paths to leave out, relative to \fI/\fR and without the leading slash; patterns with \fB*\fR and \fB?\fR work (default: not set).
.TP
.B BACKUP_INCLUDES
A comma separated list of paths, like \fBBACKUP_EXCLUDES\fR, that are backed up even when an exclude covers them (default: not set).
.TP
.B BACKUP_CLIENT_CERT
The client certificate of a system that is not part of the realm (default: not set).
Make it with \fBcreate_client_cert\fR in
.BR otpme\-ca (1),
e.g.:
.RS
.PP
.B otpme\-ca create_client_cert SITE_CA client1 cert.pem key.pem
.RE
.IP
Its CN is the name \fBALLOW_NEW_BACKUP_REPOS\fR lists.
A host of the realm uses its own certificate and needs none of these three.
.TP
.B BACKUP_CLIENT_KEY
The key of that certificate (default: not set).
.TP
.B BACKUP_CLIENT_CA
The CA certificate of the site, copied from \fI/etc/otpme/ssl/ca.pem\fR of a node (default: not set).
.hy
.ad
.SH FILES
.na
.nh
.TP
.I /etc/otpme/otpme.conf
This file.
.TP
.I ~/.otpme/otpme.conf
The config file of a user, for the parameters that say \(dqA user can set it\(dq above.
Any other parameter in it is ignored.
.TP
.I /etc/otpme/otpme.conf.dist
The file as it comes with OTPme, with all parameters and a comment on each.
pip puts it in \fIetc/otpme/\fR below its \fIsite\-packages\fR directory, not here; on install you copy it here, and to \fIotpme.conf\fR, see \fIREADME.md\fR.
.TP
\fI/etc/otpme/otpme.key\fR, \fI/etc/otpme/otpme.key_salt\fR
The master key and its salt, see \fBKEY_COMMAND\fR.
.TP
\fI/etc/otpme/backup.pass\fR, \fI/etc/otpme/backup.key\fR
The password of the repository and the key of the backup of this system, see \fBBACKUP\fR.
.TP
\fI/etc/otpme/redis.conf\fR, \fI/etc/otpme/postgresql.conf\fR, \fI/etc/otpme/mysql.cnf\fR
If there, used instead of the config OTPme writes for Redis, PostgreSQL or MySQL.
The parameters of this file for that program then do nothing.
.hy
.ad
